# Work, Energy, and Power: The Currency of Physics

> Work is force moving through a distance, energy is the capacity
> to do work, and power is how fast the work gets done. Together
> they form the accounting system of physics.

## 1. Work Done by a Force

[definition]
**Work** is done when a force acts along a displacement; it equals
force × displacement × cosine of the angle between them.
[/definition]

@math label="eq:work"
W = F s \cos\theta
@/math

The SI unit of work (and energy) is the **joule**: 1 J = 1 N·m.
Only the force component along the motion counts, so when θ = 90°
the force does no work at all.

| Angle θ | Work | Everyday picture |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 0° | positive, W = Fs | pushing a box along its path |
| 90° | zero | carrying a bag while walking level |
| 180° | negative | friction acting on a sliding box |

## 2. Kinetic Energy

@math label="eq:kinetic-energy"
KE = \frac{1}{2}mv^{2}
@/math

**Kinetic energy** is the energy of motion. The **work–energy
theorem** states that the net work done on a body equals its
change in kinetic energy — pushing a cart adds KE, friction
removes it.

[example]
Doubling the speed quadruples the energy: at 2v the kinetic
energy is ½m(2v)² = 4 × ½mv². This is why even small rises in
road speed make accidents far more destructive.
[/example]

## 3. Potential Energy

@math label="eq:potential-energy"
PE = mgh
@/math

**Potential energy** is stored energy of position or shape: a
lifted book (gravitational), a stretched bow or compressed spring
(elastic), water held behind a dam. Only the *change* in height
matters, so the floor may be chosen as h = 0 anywhere.

## 4. Conservation of Energy

[definition]
Energy can neither be created nor destroyed — it only changes
form. The total energy of an isolated system stays constant.
[/definition]

[example]
**Falling ball.** A ball dropped from 20 m starts with
PE = mgh and finishes with KE = ½mv²:

@math label="eq:falling-ball"
v = \sqrt{2gh} = \sqrt{2 \times 9.8 \times 20} \approx 20\ \mathrm{m/s}
@/math

A pendulum performs the same trade every swing: maximum PE at the
ends of the arc, maximum KE at the lowest point.
[/example]

@diagram tree id="energy-chain" layout="tree" aria="Energy transformation chain in a hydroelectric power station"
  "Dam water: gravitational PE" -> "Falling water: kinetic energy"
  "Falling water: kinetic energy" -> "Turbine: kinetic energy"
  "Turbine: kinetic energy" -> "Generator: electrical energy"
  "Generator: electrical energy" -> "Homes: light and heat"
@/diagram

## 5. Power

@math label="eq:power"
P = \frac{W}{t}
@/math

**Power** is the rate of doing work; its unit is the **watt**
(1 W = 1 J/s). The same task done faster demands more power.

[example]
A 50 kg student climbs a 4 m staircase in 10 s. Work done
= mgh = 50 × 9.8 × 4 ≈ 1960 J, so the power output is
1960 ÷ 10 ≈ 196 W — about two old light bulbs.
[/example]

## 6. The Commercial Unit: Kilowatt-Hour

Electricity bills measure energy in the **kilowatt-hour**, the
energy delivered by 1 kW running for one hour.

@math label="eq:kwh"
1\ \mathrm{kWh} = 3.6 \times 10^{6}\ \mathrm{J}
@/math

[example]
A 100 W bulb burning for 10 hours uses 100 × 10 = 1000 Wh
= 1 kWh — exactly one "unit" on a domestic electricity bill.
[/example]

## 7. Forms of Energy

| Form | Carried by | Example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Mechanical | position + motion | dam water, flying arrow |
| Heat | random molecular motion | engine radiator |
| Chemical | molecular bonds | food, petrol, battery |
| Electrical | moving charges | household supply |
| Light | electromagnetic waves | sunlight, lasers |
| Nuclear | binding energy of nuclei | uranium fission, the Sun |

Energy chains everyday: food (chemical) → muscle work
(mechanical) → heat; petrol (chemical) → engine (kinetic + heat).

## 8. Quick Revision

| Item | Remember |
| --- | --- |
| Work | W = Fs cos θ; unit joule (J) |
| Zero work | force ⊥ displacement (θ = 90°) |
| Kinetic energy | KE = ½mv²; doubling v gives 4× KE |
| Potential energy | PE = mgh |
| Conservation | energy only changes form |
| Power | P = W/t; unit watt (W) |
| Commercial unit | 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ J |
